        <p class="lecture-text">
          The ocean does not operate in isolation. It is one of five tightly coupled Earth systems — alongside the <strong>atmosphere</strong>, <strong>lithosphere</strong>, <strong>biosphere</strong>, and <strong>cryosphere</strong> — continuously exchanging energy, mass, and chemical signals across every boundary. Understanding physical oceanography at the systems level means tracing these exchanges: how solar energy drives surface heating and evaporation; how wind stress transfers momentum from atmosphere to ocean, generating currents and waves; how freshwater from the hydrological cycle dilutes surface salinity; how seafloor geology shapes basin geometry and constrains circulation pathways; and how living organisms alter chemistry, turbidity, and gas concentrations. The ocean integrates all these inputs and returns signals — heat, moisture, CO₂, nutrients — back to the systems that drive it, creating <strong>feedback loops</strong> that regulate Earth's climate on timescales from days to millennia. Human systems now constitute a sixth forcing, altering temperature, chemistry, and circulation in ways the natural system has not experienced in millions of years.
